From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 02:07:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Count the J604's eleven-row states in
 Main as macOS does

Main +0xfc0 takes the number of firmware rows. With the J604's eleven
rows that is 11, but macOS 26B123 writes 10 there on the same machine
with the same eleven rows: the states above off, the same value as the
top index in Main +0xfc4. 25G83 copies the word at init and uses it as
a bound, and as an index of 0x1800-byte blocks into the address of a
record the event handler builds, so our 11 points that record one
block further than macOS does.

Write the top index there with the eleven-row table, as macOS does. The
ten-row encoding is unchanged.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16/fw/initdata.rs | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16/fw/initdata.rs b/drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16/fw/initdata.rs
index df0d564e1e..02fbd7bc28 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16/fw/initdata.rs
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16/fw/initdata.rs
@@ -753,7 +753,16 @@ pub(crate) fn encode(
         (0xed0, super::super::params::gpu_off_delay()),
         (0xed4, 1),
         (0xed8, 8),
-        (0xfc0, input.performance.count as u32),
+        // The states above off, as macOS writes it with the J604's eleven rows (10); the ten-row
+        // encoding keeps its row count.
+        (
+            0xfc0,
+            if input.performance.count == 11 {
+                hwdata::j604::STATES
+            } else {
+                input.performance.count as u32
+            },
+        ),
         (0x259c, 1),
         (0x2544, 4),
         (0x2548, 6),
